"Al Klosterkemper" <akloster@bellsouth.net> writes:
> I'm looking at my tape right now.  It is part number AQ-PFD50-01 "MV DIAG
> MAINT TK50 1/1" with a label on top saying "Confidential Software of Digital
> Equipment Corporation" and "Valid Right-to-Use License Required."  Not sure
> what that legally means, but maybe it is still obtainable from HP.

It doesn't legally mean anything unless you're under some contractual
obligation to DEC, such as a signed license agreement, an employee
confidentiality agreement, etc.  In the absence of such contracts, you
can do whatever you like with the tape, subject to the normal legal
restrictions that apply to any copyrighted materials.  You can dispose
of the physical tape in any manner you like, such as by giving it away
or selling it.

I can stamp "confidential" and "valid right-to-use license required" all
over things that I give to other people, but in the absence of a
contract, they aren't required to pay any attention to such notices.

IANAL, though, so YMMV.


