Bill Gunshannon wrote:

> I have a VS3100 with a proken network switch.  Given the unlikeliness
> of finding a replacement switch, the difficulty in actually removing
> the old one and the fact that I would never use 10Base2 again, I propose
> to merely solder jumpers on the board to make it permanently set for AUI.
>
> So, does anyone have a schematic or info on the inside of the switch
> so I can determine what contacts get soldered together to do this??

That's the little "push-push" ("alternate action")
switch at the back of the box near the network connectors,
right?

If so, then the switching mechanism is just a series
of sliders that slide over the "posts" that are formed
by the soldered connections. Here's some ASCII art:

One position:

 <====>      <====>
   o  o  o     o  o  o
  <====>      <====>


  <====>      <====>
   o  o  o     o  o  o
  <====>      <====>


The other position:

     <====>      <====>
   o  o  o     o  o  o
     <====>      <====>

     <====>      <====>
   o  o  o     o  o  o
     <====>      <====>


Imagine that those bits shown as "o" are the posts
that stick up from the PC board. The bits shown as

  <====>

  <====>

Are actually "U" shaped sliders that slide down
over the posts and contact the posts on both sides
(so this is really a cross-section *AT* the level
of the contact points). As those U-shaped sliders
slide fore-and-aft, they short the middle post in
each SPDT triple to the front-most or rear-most post.

So just amputate the upper (moving) part of the switch
and solder the sliders (or wires) where you need them.
If the switch should be depressed, short the middle-
to-rear posts in each SPDT tripple. If the switch should
be undepressed, short the middle-to-front in each tripple.

Atlant




