>just got ahold of a
>AlphaServer 2100 4/275 (4CPU)
>AlphaServer 2000 4/200 (1CPU) <though the sticker says it's a 5/250>
>That are largely incomplete.

It's worth mentioning that those CPUs are interchangeable, i.e. you can take
2 of the 275 MHz CPUs and put them in the 2000 if you want.  You aren't
supposed to mix speeds, though, so you'd have to toss the 200 MHz.

>Is it worth it to rebuild these monsters? (the 2100 is a rackmount unit, 
>on a rack, the 2000 is a standalone)

The 2000 pedestal version is a pain in the ass to open up, compared with the
2100 pedestal.  I don't know about the rackmount.

>At the moment, they have some RAM, no HDs (no rackchassis for HDs, no 
>trays for HDs), CDs, floppys. No manuals, no OS CDs.

The 2000 takes regular SIMMs (full parity) up to 512M per memory board I
think.  So if you have a memory board you're most of the way there.  The
memory in the 2100 is permanently attached to the memory boards so you're
pretty much stuck with what's in there already.  Unless you can scrounge
more boards, that is.  The 4th CPU takes up 2 memory board slots, so moving
it to the 2000 might be worthwhile if you do find additional boards.

There should be SCSI interfaces, possibly even external ones.  You can use
any standard SCSI disk for data, but only certain ones as a system boot disk
(for VMS at least).  Anything labelled DEC which is 2GB or larger should
work.

>If they were rebuilt, what would be the best linux distro to get for them? 
>Or other OS (BSD - Free/Net/Open)... (and anyone know where I can pick up 
>OSF/1 cheaply, for these systems? and NT) (VMS?)

I think Redhat is available.  VMS is free for hobbyist use -- see the
comp.os.vms FAQ.  

>I have a suspicion that the 2000 has ARC, and the 2100 has SRM, but I 
>can't tell, since I know little about AlphaServers.

They may have both loaded, you just need to do some magic to switch between
the two.  I think ARC is what you need for Linux.  SRM for VMS.

--Keith Lewis              klewis$mitre.org
