preventing both of them from booting. The only common hardware, as far as I
know, is the disgnostic unit (the one which operates the alarn light and
buzzer), and I didn't think that could be a single point of failure.

It is likely, if the equipment has been out of service for some time, that
the memory batteries are flat, and probably dead. Have you tried leaving the
system powered up for 24 hours, then trying again.

If the batteries are not quiesced (button on the battery housing) when the
systen is shut down, then they go flat after about 100 hours. Not long after
that, they are permanently damaged.

It seems possible that badly damaged batteries could prevent the system fron
starting up. Disconecting them might be worth a try.


