> >on, there is activity on the LEDs, but it settles to an LED state
> >0-100 (4) which is documented as "Waiting for console terminal ready
> >flag". I am baffled as to how to proceed. I see nothing on the console
> >in either case.
> 
> I always found DEC picky about receiving both DTR and RTS on
> terminal interfaces -- you may need the basic 12? pins in your
> cable and null modem -- or test with a break out box or digitial
> meter to see what's generated from the interface and what's
> crossed on the null modem. 

Whoa!  The CPU shouldn't be receiving either DTR or RTS.  It's
DTE and should be *generating* those signals, if modem control
is enabled... but normally the consoles don't use modem
control all you need are TD, RD and SG (transmit, receive [which the
null modem will swap] and signal ground.)

Is this the same thread where someone recently posted the pinout
for the headers on the board?  (It is difficult to look up old posts
with Pine, or at least I haven' found out an easy way to do it,
aside from unsubscribing and then having to wade through everything
again.)  If so, ISTR that two of the pins were shorted together;
lack of this might explain the "waiting for console terminal ready
flag".  This might mean it doesn't think a cable is plugged in to
the port.

Or it could be that it is really waiting for DSR... the null modem
should be supplying this signal (and carrier detect) from the
terminal's (or the terminal emulator's) DTR signal.  Is your
terminal configured to supply modem control signals?  With a
VT2xx or later, this is a setup option.

A breakout box is really the best tool for diagnosing a problem
like this, but they seem to be getting harder to come by.

