In comp.os.vms Maverick <maverick902@hotmail.com> wrote:
>> What are (and were if you happen to know) the round-trip-times between
>> the sytsems?

> Im gonna show myself up now, but How do I measure / compute the RTT?
> Ping times are about 10 sec for 64 byte packets

Ten _seconds_ !?! That is huge - in speed-of-light terms it would be
the equivalent to going through something like five geosync satellite
links each way.

Another way to guesstimate RTTs would be with a netperf TCP_RR test
using single-byte requests and responses. I'm not sure if anyone has
netperf ported to VMS or not though.

> Also, how do I see the window size?

In a packet trace :)

>> Finally, it might be worthwhile to get a tcpdump (packet) trace of
>> a transfer that is slow and take a look at what it may show.

> I'd like to see what's happening at the alpha end.  Is there
> anything like tcpdump I can run on the alpha, or do I have to mirror
> the port and monitor it from another device?

If tcpdump has been ported to Alpha/VMS then I suspect that there will
be something to that effect on www.tcpdump.org. I myself came from
pre-merger HP and have only started up the VMS leraning curve.

Given the (lack of) speeds invovled, mirroring the port may be the
easiest way to go.

However, if your round-trip-times are _really_ 10 seconds, then to get
2 Mbit/s (that was the leased line's bitrate right?) you would need

2 Mbit/s <= W/10 seconds

or 

20 Mbit/s * 10 s <= W

which becomes

20 Mbit <= W or W at least 2.5 million bytes or ~2.4 MBytes (MB ==
1024). I am reasonably confident that no TCP/IP stack out there runs
that large a default window size. Such a large window size requires
support on both ends for TCP Window Scaling (RFC 1323). 

Without RFC 1323 support available and enabled on both ends, the
largest window in TCP is roughly 64KB, which is 524,288 bits. A window
of that many bits with a 10 second RTT would yield something like
52428 bits per second or 6.4 KByte/s of throughput. IIRC, you stated
earlier that the systems are getting 25 KB/s, which means that either
they (or the apps) have been tuned with a larger window size, or the
RTT is actually less than 10 seconds.

rick jones

And once windows get into the MB range, one probably wants SACK and
Timestamp options enabled...

