In comp.os.vms Dale King <dalek@forpresident.com> wrote:
> Maverick wrote:
>>- An FTP put from any NT box to any NT box (over the link) is  about 170kB/s
>>in both directions
>>- An FTP put from an NT box in A to the DS20 in B is again about 25kB/s

> This would indicate the TCP window size is not the problem, as it
> would be set by the source (NT box). 

The "classic" TCP window is set by the receiver.  However, the
"effective" TCP window size would be the lesser of the receiver's
classic TCP window, the senders SO_SNDBUF (or equivalent) and the
sender's calculated congestion window.

So, indeed, it does appear that there may be enough receiver window -
which leaves questions about the SO_SNDBUF (which while usually, may
not be the same as the SO_RCVBUF - in some cases FTP codes weren't
setting both) and the cwnd (congestion window).  And questions of cwnd
takes us to questions of packet loss and perhaps receiver ACK
policies, which takes us back to the "netstat" statistics and the
packet trace.

> He could try a UDP throughput test (with the TTCP utility in TCPIP
> V5.1+) to confirm this.

Or a netperf UDP_STREAM - I'll have to go email the VMS networking
guys and lobby for that one instead of ttcp :)

That would indeed show that the CPUs at either end were able to deal
with traffic rates > 25 KB/s, however, care has to be taken with the
size of the UDP datagrams - if the network does have packet loss
issues, the multiplicative increase of IP fragmentation on datagram
loss for UDP datagrams > the MTU would kick-in. It also would not
address issues relating to TCP ACK policies or SO_SNDBUF size.

rick jones

btw, if there is indeed a "netstat" on VMS, then a netstat -an (or its
equivalent) showing the sendq on the sending side would show the
SO_SNDBUF setting - that is presuming that the sending FTP is keeping
the socket buffer full all the time.

