I bought a pre-loved AlphaStation 250 4/266 with 96 MB RAM.  It has been
happily booting and running Red Hat 6.2, both for its previous owner and 
for me.  

Today I received my Noncommercial Tru64 5.1 kit.  I started by running
the firmware update (V5.8) and accepting the defaults, both for the 
file and for reflashing both SRM and ARC.  The log messages from the
reflashing looked fine;  I exited the update program, turned the box
off, waited 15 seconds and turned it back on.

Since then it has been hosed.  

If I turn it on or reset it, das blinkenlights cycle through FC, FB and F9 
forever;  there is no beep code.

If I remove enough memory that neither bank is valid then, as expected, I
get a 1-3-3 beep code, and a status code of FD, so I know that the speaker
works and the SROM is behaving somewhat rationally.

Any suggestions?

I assume that the firmware update affects the flash (SRM & ARC) but not
the SROM or the DROM -- right?

Judging by the error codes, have I fried something in the cache or the
memory system by running the flash update with the system cover off?

Or is it labouring under the delusion that it's an AlphaStation 200 and
looking for three pairs of RAM chips instead of two banks of four?

The previous SRM version was V6.3-4 (I have included the full config below);
the new version being installed was 7.0-something.  The SROM is labelled 
"030V2" "422B" (or maybe "4228"), and the DROM (not that I think it gets 
that far, much less to the flash) is labelled "DEC 94" "HA9508" "359E7".

Any clues much appreciated --

   Thomas Maslen
